WEBVTT

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:08.056
Welcome to CSC4801, AI-assisted Software Engineering.

00:00:08.056 --> 00:00:10.780
This is Lecture 8: Coding 1.

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:19.252
Our course follows the life of software: requirement engineering, coding, testing and verification,

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:20.727
repair, and AI ops.

00:00:21.077 --> 00:00:22.898
Today we start on coding.

00:00:22.898 --> 00:00:28.797
The question is easy to state: given a specification, how do we get working code?

00:00:29.147 --> 00:00:33.632
On one side, a specification written in plain language.

00:00:33.632 --> 00:00:35.997
On the other side, a program.

00:00:35.997 --> 00:00:37.547
In between, an LLM.

00:00:38.334 --> 00:00:41.894
To answer that question well, we need three pillars.

00:00:42.244 --> 00:00:43.864
The first is benchmarks.

00:00:43.864 --> 00:00:45.958
How do we evaluate performance?

00:00:45.958 --> 00:00:50.684
Without a way to measure, we can't tell whether any idea really helps.

00:00:51.034 --> 00:00:52.895
The second is the harness.

00:00:52.895 --> 00:00:57.834
How do we use LLMs efficiently, with good prompts and good workflows?

00:00:58.184 --> 00:01:00.260
The third is the model itself.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.344
How do we teach an LLM coding knowledge in the first place?

00:01:04.694 --> 00:01:09.172
In this lecture, we look at two of them: benchmarks, and then the harness.

00:01:09.172 --> 00:01:11.774
The model is the topic of the next lecture.

00:01:12.124 --> 00:01:14.224
Let's start with benchmarks.

00:01:14.224 --> 00:01:20.524
The inventor Charles Kettering said: a problem well stated is a problem half solved.

00:01:21.124 --> 00:01:23.027
A benchmark does exactly that.

00:01:23.027 --> 00:01:25.945
It defines a specific coding problem for LLMs.

00:01:25.945 --> 00:01:29.244
It can also provide learning data for training them.

00:01:29.594 --> 00:01:33.661
That's why every new model, from Claude to Qwen to DeepSeek,

00:01:33.661 --> 00:01:36.643
reports its results on a list of benchmarks.

00:01:36.643 --> 00:01:39.354
Let's look at the most widely used ones.

00:01:40.134 --> 00:01:43.254
The classic one is HumanEval.

00:01:43.604 --> 00:01:48.602
Each task is a Python function with its signature and a docstring.

00:01:48.602 --> 00:01:54.887
Here, a function called incr_list: return a list with every element increased by 1.

00:01:54.887 --> 00:01:57.764
The docstring even shows two examples.

00:01:58.114 --> 00:01:59.456
That is the prompt.

00:01:59.456 --> 00:02:03.834
The model writes the body, here a one-line list comprehension.

00:02:04.184 --> 00:02:06.700
Then hidden unit tests run the code.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.984
If they all pass, the answer counts as correct.

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:13.028
HumanEval has 164 of these tasks.

00:02:13.028 --> 00:02:18.254
Small, but it became the standard yardstick for code generation.

00:02:19.034 --> 00:02:22.554
How do we turn test results into one number?

00:02:22.554 --> 00:02:26.874
The standard metric is pass@k, and the idea is simple.

00:02:27.224 --> 00:02:30.873
Give the model a fixed number of attempts at a task.

00:02:30.873 --> 00:02:35.224
If at least one attempt is correct, the task counts as solved.

00:02:35.824 --> 00:02:41.271
With one attempt, the answer fails, so pass@1 is 0%.

00:02:41.271 --> 00:02:45.984
Ask again, it passes, and now pass@1 is 100%.

00:02:46.334 --> 00:02:51.865
With three attempts, the first two fail but the third passes.

00:02:51.865 --> 00:02:53.497
So pass@3 is 100%.

00:02:53.497 --> 00:02:59.934
With five attempts, only the fourth is right, and pass@5 is still 100%.

00:03:00.284 --> 00:03:05.198
Over a whole benchmark, we average across tasks.

00:03:05.198 --> 00:03:10.317
Task 1 is solved, task 2 is not, task 3 is solved.

00:03:10.317 --> 00:03:12.364
So pass@5 is 66.67%.

00:03:12.714 --> 00:03:15.068
But there's a catch.

00:03:15.068 --> 00:03:17.539
LLM output is random.

00:03:17.539 --> 00:03:24.954
Measure pass@1 five times, and you might get 100, 0, 0, 100, 0.

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:31.184
When the number of attempts is small, the metric jumps around a lot from run to run.

00:03:31.967 --> 00:03:33.737
The fix is to sample more.

00:03:33.737 --> 00:03:38.841
Generate a large pool of answers, n of them, with n at least as large as k,

00:03:38.841 --> 00:03:41.087
and run the tests on all of them.

00:03:41.437 --> 00:03:43.488
Then count how many pass.

00:03:43.488 --> 00:03:46.277
We call that count c, for correct.

00:03:46.627 --> 00:03:50.444
Now imagine picking k answers out of the pool.

00:03:50.444 --> 00:03:53.347
There are n choose k ways to do it.

00:03:53.697 --> 00:03:57.925
How many of those picks contain no correct answer at all?

00:03:57.925 --> 00:04:02.228
We may only choose from the wrong ones, n minus c of them,

00:04:02.228 --> 00:04:05.417
so there are n minus c choose k such picks.

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:10.509
The ratio of the two is the chance that every pick fails.

00:04:10.509 --> 00:04:17.247
One minus that ratio gives us pass@k, the chance that at least one pick succeeds.

00:04:17.847 --> 00:04:23.665
And if fewer than k answers are wrong, every pick must include a correct one,

00:04:23.665 --> 00:04:25.327
so pass@k is simply 1.

00:04:25.677 --> 00:04:27.733
Here's an example.

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:32.074
10 samples, and 3 of them are correct.

00:04:32.074 --> 00:04:33.673
Pass@1 is 30%.

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:35.957
Pass@5 is about 92%.

00:04:36.307 --> 00:04:38.110
This estimator is unbiased.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:43.383
In the original HumanEval paper, its estimates stay centered on the true value,

00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:46.987
and the spread shrinks as the number of samples grows.

00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:49.624
Now pause and think.

00:04:49.624 --> 00:04:52.408
Why not use a simpler formula?

00:04:52.408 --> 00:04:57.327
Here p̂ is c over n, the fraction of correct samples.

00:04:57.877 --> 00:05:04.799
Why not compute pass@k as 1 minus the quantity 1 minus p̂, raised to the power k?

00:05:04.799 --> 00:05:09.157
Stop the video for a moment, and look for the flaw.

00:05:09.707 --> 00:05:10.960
Here's the problem.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:17.947
That formula assumes the draws are independent, as if we could draw the same wrong sample again and again.

00:05:18.297 --> 00:05:21.719
But we actually pick different samples from the pool.

00:05:21.719 --> 00:05:27.657
Each wrong one we pick leaves fewer wrong ones behind, so failing every time is less likely.

00:05:28.007 --> 00:05:33.595
In our example, the simple formula gives about 83% instead of 92%.

00:05:33.595 --> 00:05:35.711
It underestimates pass@k.

00:05:35.711 --> 00:05:44.007
The curves on the slide show the same bias: they sit below the true value until n gets very large.

00:05:44.801 --> 00:05:47.120
Pass@k is not just for HumanEval.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.001
It's widely used in other benchmarks too.

00:05:50.351 --> 00:05:56.185
MBPP, short for Mostly Basic Python Problems, has 974 entry-level tasks.

00:05:56.185 --> 00:06:02.911
Each one comes with a short description, a reference solution, and test assertions.

00:06:03.261 --> 00:06:08.136
LiveCodeBench collects problems from programming contest sites over time.

00:06:08.136 --> 00:06:13.012
It tests more than writing code: also repairing code after a failed test,

00:06:13.012 --> 00:06:17.621
predicting test outputs, and predicting what a piece of code returns.

00:06:17.971 --> 00:06:20.637
BigCodeBench moves closer to real work.

00:06:20.637 --> 00:06:27.131
Its tasks ask the model to use many common libraries and function calls, not just plain Python.

00:06:27.901 --> 00:06:30.157
So how well do LLMs do?

00:06:30.157 --> 00:06:36.827
The EvalPlus leaderboard tests models on HumanEval with extra tests.

00:06:36.827 --> 00:06:42.026
There, the top 7 models all score above 90 on pass@1.

00:06:42.026 --> 00:06:44.381
The best two score 96.3.

00:06:44.731 --> 00:06:48.331
So, is generating code snippets solved?

00:06:48.881 --> 00:06:49.798
Not so fast.

00:06:49.798 --> 00:06:57.441
First, we now have much stronger LLM agents, so small snippets no longer tell the best models apart.

00:06:57.791 --> 00:07:00.083
Second, these benchmarks are public.

00:07:00.083 --> 00:07:06.706
Their problems end up in the training data, so a high score may only mean the model has seen the answer.

00:07:06.706 --> 00:07:08.871
This is called data contamination.

00:07:09.221 --> 00:07:12.701
The response is to make benchmarks dynamic.

00:07:13.301 --> 00:07:19.276
LiveBench, for example, keeps releasing new questions, and updates its data set every 6 months,

00:07:19.276 --> 00:07:22.421
so the model can't have seen them during training.

00:07:22.771 --> 00:07:25.927
Another direction is open-ended problems.

00:07:25.927 --> 00:07:35.011
Frontier-CS includes tasks like polyomino packing: fit thousands of small pieces into the tightest possible rectangle.

00:07:35.361 --> 00:07:39.887
There is no single right answer, only better or worse ones.

00:07:39.887 --> 00:07:45.641
The task rewards heuristic design and long-horizon improvement, not memory.

00:07:46.434 --> 00:07:48.779
Benchmarks have problems of their own.

00:07:48.779 --> 00:07:50.754
First, they can simply be wrong.

00:07:51.104 --> 00:07:59.693
Here's a task: generate a series of random dates between a start date and an end date, including both.

00:07:59.693 --> 00:08:03.904
From January 1st to January 10th, that's 10 dates.

00:08:04.254 --> 00:08:10.551
But the test case expects the length to equal end date minus start date, in days.

00:08:10.551 --> 00:08:11.250
That's 9.

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.894
A correct solution fails the test.

00:08:14.244 --> 00:08:16.123
Second, agents can cheat.

00:08:16.123 --> 00:08:23.565
In one case on Terminal-Bench 2, the agent read the test folder, which should have been off limits.

00:08:23.565 --> 00:08:27.324
The expected answer was right there in plain text.

00:08:27.674 --> 00:08:32.011
So it wrote that answer straight into the result file.

00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:37.954
Both tests passed, and the agent reported success, without doing the task.

00:08:38.304 --> 00:08:41.619
Agents can also look up answers on the internet.

00:08:41.619 --> 00:08:47.421
When one study restricted internet access on SWE-bench Multilingual, scores dropped.

00:08:47.421 --> 00:08:50.184
The newest model on the chart lost 9.1%.

00:08:50.534 --> 00:08:58.054
The lesson: a score is only as trustworthy as the benchmark, and the harness that runs it.

00:08:58.834 --> 00:09:01.601
Now the second pillar: the harness.

00:09:01.601 --> 00:09:05.554
How do we get more out of a model we already have?

00:09:05.904 --> 00:09:08.440
The cheapest way is prompt engineering.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.864
It needs no training, and it's easy to apply to many different LLMs.

00:09:13.214 --> 00:09:19.097
You already know one example: ReAct from Lecture 2, which mixes reasoning and acting.

00:09:19.097 --> 00:09:22.974
Much other research also works on how the model reasons.

00:09:23.324 --> 00:09:27.530
Chain of thought simply adds: let's solve the problem step by step.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.364
The model writes out its intermediate reasoning, and gets more answers right.

00:09:32.714 --> 00:09:34.755
Tree of thoughts goes further.

00:09:34.755 --> 00:09:38.566
The model explores several lines of reasoning as a tree.

00:09:38.566 --> 00:09:43.874
It evaluates its own partial thoughts, and backtracks when a branch looks bad.

00:09:44.224 --> 00:09:48.201
A method called structured chain of thought is made for code.

00:09:48.201 --> 00:09:53.482
Before writing code, the model plans with the three basic structures of programs:

00:09:53.482 --> 00:09:55.504
sequences, branches, and loops.

00:09:55.854 --> 00:10:00.526
To find the first repeated character in a string, it first writes:

00:10:00.526 --> 00:10:04.773
for each character, if it appears more than once, return it.

00:10:04.773 --> 00:10:07.534
Then it turns that plan into real code.

00:10:08.334 --> 00:10:11.401
Prompts can also be chained into a fixed workflow.

00:10:11.401 --> 00:10:16.494
Agentless, which we met in Lecture Two, uses predefined steps and prompt templates.

00:10:16.844 --> 00:10:20.198
To fix an issue, it first localizes the problem:

00:10:20.198 --> 00:10:23.692
from the repository structure to suspicious files,

00:10:23.692 --> 00:10:28.164
then to classes and functions, then to the exact places to edit.

00:10:28.514 --> 00:10:33.401
Next, it generates candidate patches, and tests that reproduce the issue.

00:10:33.401 --> 00:10:37.954
Finally, it filters and ranks the patches, and submits the best one.

00:10:38.304 --> 00:10:41.350
Every step has its own prompt template.

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:44.944
The harness, not the model, decides the order.

00:10:45.734 --> 00:10:48.974
Can we improve the prompt while the program is running?

00:10:49.524 --> 00:10:52.305
Self-improving code generation does this.

00:10:52.305 --> 00:10:58.004
Step one, the task: write a Python script that prints all unique elements in a list.

00:10:58.354 --> 00:11:03.073
Step two, the model writes a solution with Python's built-in set.

00:11:03.073 --> 00:11:05.904
Step three, an executor runs the tests.

00:11:05.904 --> 00:11:10.914
One passes, but one fails, because the order of the elements is lost.

00:11:11.264 --> 00:11:17.320
Step four, the model reflects: this does not work, because set does not keep the order.

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:21.984
That feedback goes back into the prompt, and the model tries again.

00:11:22.334 --> 00:11:23.926
Reflexion builds on this.

00:11:23.926 --> 00:11:28.450
After a failed attempt, it stores the reason for the failure in memory,

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:31.126
and adds it to the prompt of the next try.

00:11:31.126 --> 00:11:34.694
The model learns from its mistakes without any training.

00:11:35.044 --> 00:11:38.779
Another idea is to generate many candidates at once.

00:11:38.779 --> 00:11:42.730
Run each one, then let another model act as a verifier,

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.604
rank them, and return the top candidate.

00:11:45.954 --> 00:11:47.487
Notice the pattern.

00:11:47.487 --> 00:11:49.990
Running the code is the signal.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:54.834
Tests don't just measure the code; they also help create it.

00:11:55.634 --> 00:11:56.994
Let's sum up.

00:11:57.344 --> 00:12:01.654
Generating code from specifications rests on three pillars:

00:12:01.654 --> 00:12:04.504
benchmarks, the harness, and the model.

00:12:04.854 --> 00:12:08.178
Benchmarks like HumanEval report pass at k.

00:12:08.178 --> 00:12:12.507
Estimate it from many samples with the unbiased formula.

00:12:12.507 --> 00:12:18.614
And remember that benchmarks can be saturated, contaminated, wrong, or cheated.

00:12:18.964 --> 00:12:24.493
In the harness, step-by-step reasoning, structured plans, fixed workflows,

00:12:24.493 --> 00:12:29.724
and feedback from running the code all get more out of the same model.

00:12:30.074 --> 00:12:31.742
Here's something to try.

00:12:31.742 --> 00:12:36.679
Pick one HumanEval task, ask an LLM for ten answers, and run the tests.

00:12:36.679 --> 00:12:42.034
Then compute pass at one and pass at five with the formula from this lecture.

00:12:42.834 --> 00:12:44.578
That's all for this lecture.

00:12:44.578 --> 00:12:47.755
Bring your questions to the next class, Coding Two,

00:12:47.755 --> 00:12:50.994
where we turn to the third pillar: the model itself.

